// greyscale — measured saturation spread 1.2 — so its LUMINANCE carries every detail
// and mapping it black->PLANET.navy / white->white preserves the seal exactly. Done as
// a real image rather than a CSS filter or blend mode, because html2canvas renders the
// PDF from screen styles and does not reliably reproduce either.
const HH_BADGE_PLANET = "data:image/webp;base64,";
// THE BLUE/WHITE SEAL (Blake 8/23). Uploaded as a JPEG on a WHITE page with no alpha,
// which would have shown as a white square on the black splash — so the circle is cut
// to transparency here rather than at render time (a CSS mask would not survive
// html2canvas, the X101 rule). Used ONLY by the splash and the workspace selector;
// HH_BADGE keeps its existing value everywhere else.
// THE 2026 SEAL (Blake 8/28). Charcoal disc, blue outer ring, TRANSPARENT — 35.4%
// alpha, so it sits INTO the graphite page instead of floating on a white tile the
// way the first version did. Measured against HH_BADGE_BLUE at 44 / 54 / 74 / 96px
// before adopting: clearly better from 54px up, slightly WORSE at 44 where the ring
// collapses to a dark blob. Used at 54+ only; the small header badge keeps the old
// mark, which stays legible at that size.
//
// HH_BADGE_BLUE is left in place, not overwritten — reverting is repointing the
// call sites back, and no asset is lost either way.
// The same seal with the specular highlights pulled back (Blake 8/28: "slightly
// reduce the heavy 3D/metallic effect so it reads cleaner at sidebar size").
// Only the SHINE is flattened — the hot pixels are blended toward a local blur
// and contrast eased 6%. No hue is touched, so the blue H, the blue rim and the
// off-white ring text are the artwork's own colours. HH_SEAL_2026 keeps the full
// gloss for the large surfaces where the depth reads as depth.
// Sidebar seal: de-glossed AND de-haloed (Blake 8/28: "reduce any heavy glow so it
// feels integrated rather than floating"). The float came from a ring of low-alpha
// spill beyond the rim — 1,401 halo pixels down to 790. The ALPHA ramp is tightened,
// so anything already near-opaque is untouched: the disc, the blue rim, the H and
// the ring text are all exactly as drawn. Only the spill goes.
// Sidebar seal: de-glossed, de-haloed, and CROPPED to the badge (Blake 8/28).
// The asset carried 8.6% transparent padding, so a 113px container rendered a
// 103px badge and every size instruction was off by that margin. The canvas is
// now the badge, squared so the circle cannot distort — container size and
// visible diameter are finally the same number.
// Sidebar seal: de-glossed, de-haloed, cropped, and the RING LETTERING lifted
// (Blake 8/29 — the words were unreadable at sidebar size). The lift is a
// LUMINANCE move inside the ring band only: letters ~#D6D5D5 -> ~#C8CCD0, the
// ring ground a shade up. No geometry is redrawn and no hue is shifted; the H
// sits inside the band and is byte-identical, which is asserted in the build.
// COMPACT SIDEBAR BADGE. The readability problem is stroke WEIGHT at 4-5 physical
// pixels, not colour — so the ring strokes are dilated 53% thicker and set to
// #EEF1F4 on a #292D32 ring, with the inner field lifted toward #1B1F24.
//
// The letterforms are NOT redrawn: a morphological dilate thickens what is
// already there. Letter-SPACING cannot be changed this way — that needs the type
// re-set, which is a design task on Blake's mark, not an image operation. The H
// and the blue rim are verified byte-identical.
// COMPACT SIDEBAR BADGE. Two operations, both on the ring band only, neither a
// redraw:
//   1. a morphological dilate thickens what is already there (+53% stroke)
//   2. a RADIAL REMAP samples the band from a narrower source annulus, which
//      stretches its contents outward — the glyphs render +25% larger
// Wording #EEF1F4 on a #292D32 ring, inner field toward #1B1F24. The H and the
// blue rim are verified byte-identical after both passes.
//
// Letter-SPACING is still the one thing this cannot do: tightening the tracking
// means re-setting the type, which is a design task on the mark itself.
// COMPACT SIDEBAR BADGE. Three operations, all on the RING BAND, none a redraw:
//   1. a morphological dilate thickens the existing strokes (+53% weight)
//   2. the INNER SEPARATOR is removed. Measured by radius, that ring peaked at
//      211 mean luminance — BRIGHTER than the lettering itself, so it competed
//      with the words and ate radial room they could use.
//   3. a RADIAL REMAP samples the band from a narrower source annulus, spreading
//      the glyphs across the freed width so they render larger.
// Wording #EEF1F4 on a #292D32 ring. The H and the blue rim are verified
// byte-identical after all three.
//
// Letter-SPACING is still the one thing this cannot do: tightening the tracking
// means re-setting the type, which is a design task on the mark itself.
// COMPACT SIDEBAR BADGE. Rebuilt from the clean crop, NOT from the earlier
// processed version — that had a +53% stroke dilate stacked on a +25% glyph
// enlargement, and the two together made the lettering fat and crowded. Blake
// called it blown out, correctly.
//
// Two operations now, both on the ring band, neither a redraw:
//   1. the INNER SEPARATOR is removed. Measured by radius it peaked at 211 mean
//      luminance — BRIGHTER than the wording it sat beside.
//   2. a RADIAL REMAP samples the band from a narrower source annulus, spreading
//      the glyphs into the freed width. Size comes from the remap alone; the
//      stroke weight is exactly as drawn. NO DILATE.
// Wording #EEF1F4 on a #292D32 ring. The H and the blue rim are verified
// byte-identical.
//
// Letter-SPACING remains the one thing this cannot do: tightening the tracking
// means re-setting the type, which is a design task on the mark itself.
// SIDEBAR BADGE, rebuilt from the MASTER artwork Blake supplied 8/29 rather than
// from my earlier processing chain. Colour, geometry and the H come straight
// from his file.
//
// Background knocked out with an EXACT CIRCULAR MASK, not a luminance threshold:
// the disc is a true circle, so the mask is precise and the blue rim keeps its
// own antialiasing. No fringing, no halo.
//
// Then two operations on the ring band, neither a redraw:
//   1. the INNER SEPARATOR removed — measured by radius it spiked to 94 mean
//      luminance against a ~20 field, the brightest thing beside the wording
//   2. a RADIAL REMAP spreads the glyphs into the freed width. Size comes from
//      the remap alone. NO DILATE — stacking one on the other is what made the
